:: NPC offer food
<h1 class="ptitle">BASEMENT</h1>
<br />
<<set _heOrShe = setup.pronounceWhat($tmpGirl)>>
<<set _hisOrHer = setup.pronounceWhos($tmpGirl)>>
<<set _himOrHer = setup.pronounceWho($tmpGirl)>>
<<set _begLinkText = 'Make ' + _himOrHer + ' beg for it'>>
<<set $tmpGirl.offeredFood = true>>

The stairs to the basement are narrow and the air turns cold before you reach the bottom. You push open the heavy door and the single overhead bulb fills the cell with pale, flat light. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> hears you on the stairs — already turned toward the door when you step through, <<=_hisOrHer>> eyes going straight to what you're carrying. The attention is immediate and total. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> studies what you've brought without appearing to study it, already working out what kind of day this is going to be.
<br /><br />

<div id="opt_scraps">
	<<linkreplace 'Left over scraps from the kitchen'>>
		<<run $('#opt_plate, #opt_plate_desc, #opt_scraps_desc').hide()>>
		You set down what was left from the kitchen after the meal had cleared. The portions are uneven, things nobody went back for. Edible. Nourishing. But plainly what remained when the better parts were taken.
		<br /><br />
		<<set _mcLine = setup.getRandomElement(["It's better than wasting it.", "You look like you need this.", "You've been behaving yourself.", "You're looking thin.", "I keep what's mine in decent shape."])>>
		<<you>>
			<<=_mcLine>>
		<</you>>
		<br /><br />
		<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> looks at the food, then at you — reading your face more than what you brought. Not performing gratitude, just collecting information. After a moment <<=_heOrShe>> steps forward and takes it, careful not to seem too eager.
		<br /><br />
		<<set _npcLines = ["Thank you.", "You didn't have to come down.", "I wasn't sure you'd bring anything today.", "It's more than I had.", "I'll eat."]>>
		<<sayNpc $tmpGirl>><<=setup.getRandomElement(_npcLines)>><</sayNpc>>
		<br />
		<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> holds the food but doesn't start eating yet. <<=_hisOrHer>> attention stays on you, reading whether you intend to stay and what staying would mean.
		<br /><br />
		<div id="opt_freely">
			<<linkreplace 'Offer it freely'>>
				<<run $('#opt_beg, #opt_freely_desc, #opt_beg_desc').hide()>>
				<<set $tmpGirl.relationship = Math.min($tmpGirl.relationship + 10, 100)>>
				<<set $tmpGirl.sub = Math.min($tmpGirl.sub + 5, 100)>>
				<<set $tmpGirl.happy = Math.min($tmpGirl.happy + 5, 100)>>
				<<set $player.goodwill += 5>>
				You set it in <<=_hisOrHer>> reach and step back.
				<br /><br />
				<<set _mcLine = setup.getRandomElement(["I look after what's mine.", "You've been looking hungry lately.", "You've been keeping your head down. I notice that.", "You're looking thin. Eat.", "You've been good. Consider it acknowledged."])>>
				<<you>>
					<<=_mcLine>>
				<</you>>
				<br /><br />
				<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> takes it. The look on <<=_hisOrHer>> face is careful, trying to place what the gesture means. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> expected something, or at least knew that something could be expected, and the plainness of it hasn't settled yet.
				<br /><br />
				<<set _npcLines = ["...Thank you.", "I wasn't sure what to expect.", "I'll make it last.", "I appreciate it.", "I didn't think you'd come today."]>>
				<<sayNpc $tmpGirl>><<=setup.getRandomElement(_npcLines)>><</sayNpc>>
				<br />
				<<if ($tmpGirl.likesGuys ?? true) && $tmpGirl.relationship > 0>>
					<<if $tmpGirl.relationship < 20>>
						<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> is appreciative, but eyes you warily. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> knows little comes in this world without a price, and there is a begrudging air of acceptance that you will do as you wish.
					<<elseif $tmpGirl.relationship < 50>>
						In <<=_hisOrHer>> gratitude <<=_heOrShe>> looks receptive — not specifically inviting, but well aware that quid-pro-quo is the way of this world, and if that's what you want, the kindness was worth it.
					<<else>>
						Along with <<=_hisOrHer>> gratitude, <<=_heOrShe>> looks inviting, <<=_hisOrHer>> body language making clear that <<=_heOrShe>> is willing to reciprocate.
					<</if>>
					<br /><br />
					<div id="btn_enter">
						<<linkreplace 'Enter the cell'>>
							<<run $('#btn_leave_freely').hide()>>
							<<if $tmpGirl.relationship < 20>>
								You step into the cell, letting the door close behind you. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> watches your approach without retreating — jaw set, eyes steady. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> knew this was coming the moment you set down the food. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> holds <<=_hisOrHer>> ground and waits, accepting what follows with the quiet resignation of someone who has stopped expecting anything free.
							<<elseif $tmpGirl.relationship < 50>>
								You step into the cell, letting the door close behind you. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> watches you with clear, steady eyes — not surprised, not resistant. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> understood the exchange from the moment you handed it over. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> shifts <<=_hisOrHer>> weight slightly and waits, willing enough to meet you halfway.
							<<else>>
								You step into the cell, letting the door close behind you. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> meets your eyes as you cross the room, something warmer than gratitude in <<=_hisOrHer>> expression. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> moves toward you — not waiting, not needing to be told — and offers <<=_himOrHer>>self with the ease of someone who genuinely wants to give something back.
							<</if>>
							<br /><br />
							<<link 'Continue'>>
								<<drop $backpack 'food' 1>>
								<<set $tmpGirlViewBack = 'NPC view'>>
								<<fuck $tmpGirl>>
							<</link>>
						<</linkreplace>>
					</div>
				<</if>>
				<div id="btn_leave_freely">
					<<link 'Leave'>>
						<<drop $backpack 'food' 1>>
						<<goto 'NPC view'>>
					<</link>>
					<div class="link-desc">Relationship +10 &bull; Submission +5 &bull; Happiness +5 &bull; Goodwill +5</div>
				</div>
			<</linkreplace>>
			<<linkdesc 'opt_freely_desc'>>
				Happiness +5 &bull; Relationship +5 &bull; Goodwill +5
			<</linkdesc>>
		</div>
		<div id="opt_beg">
			<<linkreplace _begLinkText>>
				<<run $('#opt_freely, #opt_freely_desc, #opt_beg_desc').hide()>>
				You hold the portion where <<=_heOrShe>> can see it but don't pass it through. You let the pause do its work. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> reads the hesitation correctly and goes still.
				<br /><br />
				<<set _mcLine = setup.getRandomElement(["Then get on your knees and ask for it.", "You know what you have to do.", "Kneel. Ask properly.", "If you want it, show me you want it.", "You'd better beg for it."])>>
				<<you>>
					<<=_mcLine>>
				<</you>>
				<br /><br />
				<<if setup.percentageChance($tmpGirl.sub * 2)>>
					<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> hesitates. Then slowly sinks to <<=_hisOrHer>> knees on the cold concrete, hands open, head bowed. The words come out low and careful. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> knows exactly what this costs.
					<br /><br />
					<<set _npcLines = ["Please. I'll do anything. Just let me eat.", "Please. I know what I am here. I'm asking you.", "I'm begging you. Please.", "I won't cause trouble. I promise. Please.", "Please. I'm yours. Just... please."]>>
					<<sayNpc $tmpGirl>><<=setup.getRandomElement(_npcLines)>><</sayNpc>>
					<br />
					<<set $tmpGirl.relationship = Math.min($tmpGirl.relationship + 10, 100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.sub = Math.min($tmpGirl.sub + 10, 100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.happy = Math.min($tmpGirl.happy + 3, 100)>>
					<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> looks submissive and receptive, understanding that <<=_hisOrHer>> body is yours — to feed or to use as you please.
					<br /><br />
					<div id="btn_enter">
						<<linkreplace 'Enter the cell'>>
							<<run $('#btn_leave_beg').hide()>>
							You step into the cell. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> is already waiting — head slightly lowered, posture open. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> doesn't speak. The food was enough to show <<=_himOrHer>> where things stand, and <<=_heOrShe>> has made <<=_hisOrHer>> peace with it. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> offers <<=_himOrHer>>self quietly, understanding that <<=_hisOrHer>> body belongs to you — to feed or to use as you see fit.
							<br /><br />
							<<link 'Continue'>>
								<<drop $backpack 'food' 1>>
								<<set $tmpGirlViewBack = 'NPC view'>>
								<<fuck $tmpGirl>>
							<</link>>
						<</linkreplace>>
					</div>
					<div id="btn_leave_beg">
						<<link 'Leave'>>
							<<drop $backpack 'food' 1>>
							<<goto 'NPC view'>>
						<</link>>
						<div class="link-desc">Relationship +10 &bull; Submission +10 &bull; Happiness +3</div>
					</div>
				<<else>>
					<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> goes still. A long moment passes. Then <<=_heOrShe>> turns away and moves to the corner of the cell, putting <<=_hisOrHer>> back to you.
					<br /><br />
					<<set _npcLines = ["Keep it.", "I won't.", "Not for that.", "I'd rather go hungry.", "Do what you want. I'm not begging."]>>
					<<sayNpc $tmpGirl>><<=setup.getRandomElement(_npcLines)>><</sayNpc>>
					<br />
					<<set $tmpGirl.relationship = Math.min($tmpGirl.relationship + 5, 100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.sub = Math.min($tmpGirl.sub + 5, 100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.happy = Math.max($tmpGirl.happy - 10, -100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.relationship = Math.max($tmpGirl.relationship - 5, -100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.sub = Math.max($tmpGirl.sub - 3, 0)>>
					<<set $player.goodwill -= 5>>
					<<set _rackLinkText = 'Take ' + _himOrHer + ' out for punishment'>>
					<div id="btn_punish">
						<<linkreplace `_rackLinkText`>>
							<<run $('#btn_forced, #btn_leave_fail').hide()>>
							You pull open the cell door and step inside. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> backs against the far wall but there is nowhere to go — you take hold of <<=_himOrHer>> before <<=_heOrShe>> can offer any real resistance and pull <<=_himOrHer>> out toward the dungeon area. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> struggles against your grip but is no match for you. You secure <<=_himOrHer>> in place, binding <<=_hisOrHer>> arms and positioning <<=_himOrHer>> for what comes next. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> goes still, bracing for the consequences of <<=_hisOrHer>> defiance.
							<br /><br />
							<<link 'Continue'>>
								<<goto 'NPC punish'>>
							<</link>>
						<</linkreplace>>
					</div>
					<div id="btn_forced">
						<<linkreplace 'Enter the cell (forced)'>>
							<<run $('#btn_punish, #btn_leave_fail').hide()>>
							You push open the cell door and step inside, the look on your face saying everything. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> reads it immediately — <<=_hisOrHer>> eyes go wide, breath catching as <<=_heOrShe>> backs away. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> puts up a fight, hands against your chest, trying to twist free — but <<=_heOrShe>> is no match for you. You press <<=_himOrHer>> down and make it clear, in terms <<=_heOrShe>> cannot mistake, what it means to belong to you.
							<br /><br />
							<<link 'Continue'>>
								<<set $tmpGirlViewBack = 'NPC view'>>
								<<set $sexForced = true>>
								<<fuck $tmpGirl>>
							<</link>>
						<</linkreplace>>
					</div>
					<div id="btn_leave_fail">
						<<link 'Leave'>>
							<<goto 'NPC view'>>
						<</link>>
						<div class="link-desc">Happiness -10 &bull; Submission +2 &bull; Goodwill -5</div>
					</div>
				<</if>>
			<</linkreplace>>
			<<linkdesc 'opt_beg_desc'>>
				Happiness ?? &bull; Relationship ?? &bull; Submission ??
			<</linkdesc>>
		</div>
	<</linkreplace>>
	<<linkdesc 'opt_scraps_desc'>>
		Relationship +5 &bull; Submission +5
	<</linkdesc>>
</div>
<div id="opt_plate">
	<<linkreplace "A fresh plate from the day's meals">>
		<<run $('#opt_scraps, #opt_scraps_desc, #opt_plate_desc').hide()>>
		You set down a full portion. The same as any other resident receives today, nothing held back and nothing added — but it is considerably more than <<=_heOrShe>> has gotten used to expecting. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> goes still when it's close enough to see clearly.
		<br /><br />
		<<set _mcLine = setup.getRandomElement(["Same as everyone else.", "Eat. It's hot.", "Don't read into it.", "It wasn't sitting right, leaving you with nothing.", "You need to keep your strength."])>>
		<<you>>
			<<=_mcLine>>
		<</you>>
		<br /><br />
		<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> stares at the plate, then at you. <<=_hisOrHer>> expression doesn't quite know where to land. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> reaches out carefully with one hand, touching the edge as if checking it's real, then draws it closer without letting go.
		<br /><br />
		<<set _npcLines = ["That's... the same as what the others get.", "You didn't have to do this.", "I don't understand.", "Is this from your own meal?", "This is more than I expected."]>>
		<<sayNpc $tmpGirl>><<=setup.getRandomElement(_npcLines)>><</sayNpc>>
		<br />
		<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> holds the plate but doesn't eat. <<=_hisOrHer>> eyes come back to your face, trying to work out what it cost you to bring this down and what, if anything, comes with it.
		<br /><br />
		<div id="opt_freely">
			<<linkreplace 'Offer it freely'>>
				<<run $('#opt_beg, #opt_freely_desc, #opt_beg_desc').hide()>>
				<<set $tmpGirl.relationship = Math.min($tmpGirl.relationship + 12, 100)>>
				<<set $tmpGirl.happy = Math.min($tmpGirl.happy + 8, 100)>>
				<<set $player.goodwill += 5>>
				You pass it through and say nothing else. You take a step back.
				<br /><br />
				<<set _mcLine = setup.getRandomElement(["I wanted you eating properly.", "The others aren't getting this.", "You've been doing better. I notice that.", "I noticed the improvement. Eat well.", "You've earned a proper meal."])>>
				<<you>>
					<<=_mcLine>>
				<</you>>
				<br /><br />
				Something shifts in <<=_hisOrHer>> expression. Not quite relief and not quite gratitude yet, but the beginning of both. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> pulls the plate closer and is quiet for a moment, composing <<=_hisOrHer>> face into something more measured.
				<br /><br />
				<<set _npcLines = ["Thank you. I mean it.", "I didn't expect this.", "...You didn't have to.", "I've been trying to do better.", "I won't forget this."]>>
				<<sayNpc $tmpGirl>><<=setup.getRandomElement(_npcLines)>><</sayNpc>>
				<br />
				<<if ($tmpGirl.likesGuys ?? true) && $tmpGirl.relationship > 0>>
					<<if $tmpGirl.relationship < 20>>
						<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> is appreciative, but eyes you warily. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> knows little comes in this world without a price, and there is a begrudging air of acceptance that you will do as you wish.
					<<elseif $tmpGirl.relationship < 50>>
						In <<=_hisOrHer>> gratitude <<=_heOrShe>> looks receptive — not specifically inviting, but well aware that quid-pro-quo is the way of this world, and if that's what you want, the kindness was worth it.
					<<else>>
						Along with <<=_hisOrHer>> gratitude, <<=_heOrShe>> looks inviting, <<=_hisOrHer>> body language making clear that <<=_heOrShe>> is willing to reciprocate.
					<</if>>
					<br /><br />
					<div id="btn_enter">
						<<linkreplace 'Enter the cell'>>
							<<run $('#btn_leave_freely').hide()>>
							<<if $tmpGirl.relationship < 20>>
								You step into the cell, letting the door close behind you. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> watches your approach without retreating — jaw set, eyes steady. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> knew this was coming the moment you set down the food. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> holds <<=_hisOrHer>> ground and waits, accepting what follows with the quiet resignation of someone who has stopped expecting anything free.
							<<elseif $tmpGirl.relationship < 50>>
								You step into the cell, letting the door close behind you. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> watches you with clear, steady eyes — not surprised, not resistant. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> understood the exchange from the moment you handed it over. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> shifts <<=_hisOrHer>> weight slightly and waits, willing enough to meet you halfway.
							<<else>>
								You step into the cell, letting the door close behind you. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> meets your eyes as you cross the room, something warmer than gratitude in <<=_hisOrHer>> expression. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> moves toward you — not waiting, not needing to be told — and offers <<=_himOrHer>>self with the ease of someone who genuinely wants to give something back.
							<</if>>
							<br /><br />
							<<link 'Continue'>>
								<<drop $backpack 'food' 1>>
								<<set $tmpGirlViewBack = 'NPC view'>>
								<<fuck $tmpGirl>>
							<</link>>
						<</linkreplace>>
					</div>
				<</if>>
				<div id="btn_leave_freely">
					<<link 'Leave'>>
						<<drop $backpack 'food' 1>>
						<<goto 'NPC view'>>
					<</link>>
					<div class="link-desc">Relationship +12 &bull; Happiness +8 &bull; Goodwill +5</div>
				</div>
			<</linkreplace>>
			<<linkdesc 'opt_freely_desc'>>
				Happiness +5 &bull; Relationship +5 &bull; Goodwill +5
			<</linkdesc>>
		</div>
		<div id="opt_beg">
			<<linkreplace _begLinkText>>
				<<run $('#opt_freely, #opt_freely_desc, #opt_beg_desc').hide()>>
				You set the plate where <<=_heOrShe>> can see it — close enough to smell — but don't pass it through. You wait. The quality of what's there is obvious, and so is the fact that it hasn't moved.
				<br /><br />
				<<set _mcLine = setup.getRandomElement(["This kind of meal has a price. You know what it is.", "Kneel. Ask me properly.", "If you want it, you beg for it.", "You know what to do first.", "Show me you understand your place, and it's yours."])>>
				<<you>>
					<<=_mcLine>>
				<</you>>
				<br /><br />
				<<if setup.percentageChance($tmpGirl.sub * 2)>>
					<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> goes quiet. Then slowly drops to <<=_hisOrHer>> knees on the cold concrete, hands folded, head down. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> is willing to give anything just to survive another day.
					<br /><br />
					<<set _npcLines = ["Please. I'll do whatever you ask. Just... please.", "Please. I know what this is. I know what I am. Please.", "I'm begging you. Please let me have it.", "Please. I'm yours. I know that. Just let me eat.", "I'll be good. I'll do anything. Please."]>>
					<<sayNpc $tmpGirl>><<=setup.getRandomElement(_npcLines)>><</sayNpc>>
					<br />
					<<set $tmpGirl.relationship = Math.min($tmpGirl.relationship + 12, 100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.happy = Math.min($tmpGirl.happy + 6, 100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.sub = Math.min($tmpGirl.sub + 5, 100)>>
					<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> looks submissive and receptive, understanding that <<=_hisOrHer>> body is yours — to feed or to use as you please.
					<br /><br />
					<div id="btn_enter">
						<<linkreplace 'Enter the cell'>>
							<<run $('#btn_leave_beg').hide()>>
							You step into the cell. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> is already waiting — head slightly lowered, posture open. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> doesn't speak. The food was enough to show <<=_himOrHer>> where things stand, and <<=_heOrShe>> has made <<=_hisOrHer>> peace with it. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> offers <<=_himOrHer>>self quietly, understanding that <<=_hisOrHer>> body belongs to you — to feed or to use as you see fit.
							<br /><br />
							<<link 'Continue'>>
								<<drop $backpack 'food' 1>>
								<<set $tmpGirlViewBack = 'NPC view'>>
								<<fuck $tmpGirl>>
							<</link>>
						<</linkreplace>>
					</div>
					<div id="btn_leave_beg">
						<<link 'Leave'>>
							<<drop $backpack 'food' 1>>
							<<goto 'NPC view'>>
						<</link>>
						<div class="link-desc">Relationship +12 &bull; Happiness +6 &bull; Submission +5</div>
					</div>
				<<else>>
					The plate sits between you. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> looks at it, then at you. <<=_hisOrHer>> jaw sets and <<=_heOrShe>> steps back to the far corner, hands at <<=_hisOrHer>> sides.
					<br /><br />
					<<set _npcLines = ["I'm not doing that.", "Keep it.", "Not for anything.", "I'd rather starve.", "You can have it back."]>>
					<<sayNpc $tmpGirl>><<=setup.getRandomElement(_npcLines)>><</sayNpc>>
					<br />
					<<set $tmpGirl.relationship = Math.min($tmpGirl.relationship + 7, 100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.happy = Math.min($tmpGirl.happy + 3, 100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.happy = Math.max($tmpGirl.happy - 10, -100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.relationship = Math.max($tmpGirl.relationship - 5, -100)>>
					<<set $tmpGirl.sub = Math.max($tmpGirl.sub - 3, 0)>>
					<<set $player.goodwill -= 5>>
					<<set _rackLinkText = 'Take ' + _himOrHer + ' out for punishment'>>
					<div id="btn_punish">
						<<linkreplace `_rackLinkText`>>
							<<run $('#btn_forced, #btn_leave_fail').hide()>>
							You pull open the cell door and step inside. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> backs against the far wall but there is nowhere to go — you take hold of <<=_himOrHer>> before <<=_heOrShe>> can offer any real resistance and pull <<=_himOrHer>> out toward the dungeon area. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> struggles against your grip but is no match for you. You secure <<=_himOrHer>> in place, binding <<=_hisOrHer>> arms and positioning <<=_himOrHer>> for what comes next. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> goes still, bracing for the consequences of <<=_hisOrHer>> defiance.
							<br /><br />
							<<link 'Continue'>>
								<<goto 'NPC punish'>>
							<</link>>
						<</linkreplace>>
					</div>
					<div id="btn_forced">
						<<linkreplace 'Enter the cell (forced)'>>
							<<run $('#btn_punish, #btn_leave_fail').hide()>>
							You push open the cell door and step inside, the look on your face saying everything. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> reads it immediately — <<=_hisOrHer>> eyes go wide, breath catching as <<=_heOrShe>> backs away. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> puts up a fight, hands against your chest, trying to twist free — but <<=_heOrShe>> is no match for you. You press <<=_himOrHer>> down and make it clear, in terms <<=_heOrShe>> cannot mistake, what it means to belong to you.
							<br /><br />
							<<link 'Continue'>>
								<<set $tmpGirlViewBack = 'NPC view'>>
								<<set $sexForced = true>>
								<<fuck $tmpGirl>>
							<</link>>
						<</linkreplace>>
					</div>
					<div id="btn_leave_fail">
						<<link 'Leave'>>
							<<goto 'NPC view'>>
						<</link>>
						<div class="link-desc">Relationship +2 &bull; Happiness -7 &bull; Submission -3 &bull; Goodwill -5</div>
					</div>
				<</if>>
			<</linkreplace>>
			<<linkdesc 'opt_beg_desc'>>
				Happiness ?? &bull; Relationship ?? &bull; Submission ??
			<</linkdesc>>
		</div>
	<</linkreplace>>
	<<linkdesc 'opt_plate_desc'>>
		Relationship +7 &bull; Happiness +3
	<</linkdesc>>
</div>
